      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t+\\pi) & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =3{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})]=-3\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & =3{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{4})]=3\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$",
