            {
              ":answer/value": "t =-9",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "53aef339-706d-540f-a495-e7866077d8e6"
            },
            {
              ":answer/value": "t = 3",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "a9e12641-25e7-545b-b70e-047144bbf6cd"
            },
            {
              ":answer/value": "t =-3",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "a5f1eab6-675b-554d-b8cf-dcde62ded709"
            },
            {
              ":answer/value": "t = 5",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "e375a73e-c7cc-5112-a366-e7ae9016d821"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "4bfab770-7f18-5d48-a93e-438242655d55",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "e375a73e-c7cc-5112-a366-e7ae9016d821"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4,\\qquad y'(t)=-9{t}^{2}+90t-225.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{t}^{2}+90t-225 & =0 \\\\ -9(t-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(5) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=5$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "76bc61c8-b6d6-51e1-8812-8d026c4f1340"
    },
