    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4,\\,y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/id": "809ea507-efb5-517b-bbcf-a143e22c1ee2"
            },
            {
              ":answer/value": "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\pi $",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/id": "74f6ccec-899b-5ff2-bc45-18b28fe5a3b6"
            },
            {
              ":answer/value": "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/id": "04715c85-82e1-511b-b6cf-d62b68bfc374"
            },
            {
              ":answer/value": "$t=0$ and $t=\\pi $",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/id": "fcf073ad-4ba8-54a8-a60b-be9a72cc3f90"
            },
            {
              ":answer/value": "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\pi $",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/id": "bceea646-cc95-541c-8e17-eff465091278"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "abb65616-e01c-5aec-b8de-cbcfa03a4cfd",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "fcf073ad-4ba8-54a8-a60b-be9a72cc3f90"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =-5\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =5\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\pi .$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "a4d85493-911a-5cc9-8df4-5363c53cb2dd"
    },
