    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t+2,\\,y={t}^{3}+\\frac{19}{2}{t}^{2}+20t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "-5",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "83ae68c4-92e5-51d1-b4f0-d790d08d814c"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "68f56a4c-ba5e-5624-96f2-5194dea41099",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "83ae68c4-92e5-51d1-b4f0-d790d08d814c"
          ]
        },
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-2",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "-\\frac{4}{3}",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "d74f4a23-611a-5147-b73e-e3a84de5771b"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "8a28fa93-8659-532e-a729-a26240011a65",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "d74f4a23-611a-5147-b73e-e3a84de5771b"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t-1,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+19t+20\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+19t+20 & =0 \\\\ (3t+4)(t+5) & =0 \\\\ t & =-5,-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-5) & =2(-5)-1=-11\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(-\\frac{4}{3}) & =2(-\\frac{4}{3})-1=-\\frac{11}{3}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-5$ and $t=-\\frac{4}{3}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "747cf572-5d39-5626-8aff-baf1595a1baf"
    },
