  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186083278,
      "id": "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}),\\,y=3{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{\\pi }{4}",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "5ab0d925-e9d7-5a68-9e4f-2e0488be353d"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "06a52ae0-3e48-5313-a426-7fc09ab90bf4",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "5ab0d925-e9d7-5a68-9e4f-2e0488be353d"
          ]
        },
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-2",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "f58941ec-7663-5141-9f10-34cb2eb752ab"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "0e7db93a-d178-5406-99f3-dad85b9061e3",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "f58941ec-7663-5141-9f10-34cb2eb752ab"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-2{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi}{2})=-1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-2{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})]=2\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & =-2{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})]=-2\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "3cb04661-02e2-5a40-960d-e85766a264d9"
    },
