      "id": "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi),\\,y=-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "786bb258-2af2-5889-bdb0-269ba632a750"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "e0324cf1-c62e-5870-b69d-7c33d0023b2e",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "786bb258-2af2-5889-bdb0-269ba632a750"
          ]
        },
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-2",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "3d214310-b660-5463-8785-c8c4b17f8927"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "e5354546-efee-5789-9d45-8e7fbf3f2c93",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "3d214310-b660-5463-8785-c8c4b17f8927"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+\\pi)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+\\pi)} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t+\\pi)=-1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =-{e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & =-{e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "24fa23ee-ef33-541b-b8de-c0b3796f5b22"
    },
