          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=-3\\pi ,-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-3\\pi ) & =5\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(-2\\pi ) & =-5\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=-3\\pi $ and $t=-2\\pi .$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "f8021cdf-cc38-5ea8-b35e-56faeccb3654"
    },
