        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\qquad y'(t)=6{t}^{2}+24t+24.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}+24t+24 & =0 \\\\ 6(t+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-2) & =1\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-2$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "b5ac1c7f-b71d-5888-820f-7565b98004db"
    },
