  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186083278,
      "id": "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-3t-\\frac{3}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-\\frac{19}{2}{t}^{2}+20t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{4}{3}",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "2734e9f6-17a2-5f16-ab2e-b198704ad9b5"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "7fe77c01-ac66-5a32-86e6-d822bac7179c",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "2734e9f6-17a2-5f16-ab2e-b198704ad9b5"
          ]
        },
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-2",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "5",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "69a83754-3f1a-5ef6-bbe2-4ff10dda3167"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "80899225-b243-5426-84b9-d6f0318f2a38",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "69a83754-3f1a-5ef6-bbe2-4ff10dda3167"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-19t+20\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-19t+20 & =0 \\\\ (3t-4)(t-5) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{4}{3},5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{4}{3}) & =2(\\frac{4}{3})-3=-\\frac{1}{3}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(5) & =2(5)-3=7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{4}{3}$ and $t=5.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/id": "2016d9f6-22a6-56bc-ac31-bf17a0eb32db"
    },
